    <p>Rectangles show tone duration, not emotion or loudness. Event IDs and beat offsets identify the same positions at every tempo. Scroll horizontally on a narrow screen.</p><div id="event-table" class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Selected event values">${eventTable(a,'short',90)}</div>
    <details class="static-guide"><summary>Compare both full event tables without audio</summary><p>At 90 BPM, short tones last 0.08 s and sustained tones 0.28 s. The complete span is 5⅓ s. The final sustained tone ends at offset 7.42, leaving silence to offset 8; no release tail exceeds the span.</p>${a.versions.map(v=>`<div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="${esc(v.label)} static table">${eventTable(a,v.id,90)}</div>`).join('')}<p>At 60 BPM, the same offsets occur at b seconds; at 120 BPM, at b/2 seconds. Tone lengths also scale. Comparing beat offsets aligns the phrase; identical clock times do not.</p><p>Cite an event, describe the duration or speed change, then propose two possible expressive effects with reasons. There is no mood answer key. The event route supports structural comparison but does not reproduce hearing.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Original stimulus and release terms</summary><p>${esc(a.assetRights.provenance)}</p><p>${esc(a.assetRights.releaseProposal)}</p><p>The WAV is mono 16-bit PCM at 48 kHz, generated locally from the reviewed event/envelope formula. Its samples use a smooth 5 ms attack and 15 ms release. No third-party recording, external instrument sample or human performance is included. The export carries its exact events, synthesis settings, renderer version and pending release terms in an embedded JSON comment. Keep that metadata with the file; audio editors may strip it. Playback volume and private notes are excluded.</p></details>`;
  return '';
}

